--TEST--
A `forget` belongs to the tier it was named on: the two in-process tiers hold two entries of one name, and forgetting on either leaves the other's where it was — `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers` gives a member per tier so that the store a
// program meant is visible where it wrote it. That has to hold for every
// operation, not only for the write: a `forget` that reached both stores would
// make one member's call an effect on the other's entries.
var $local = Core\Cache::local();
var $process = Core\Cache::process();

$local->put("shared-name", "the core's own");
$process->put("shared-name", "the process's own");

$local->forget("shared-name");
echo "forgotten on the local tier: ", $local->get("shared-name") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";
var $kept = ($process->get("shared-name") ?? "") as string;
echo "the process tier still has: ", $kept, "\n";

// And the other way round, so that neither tier is merely the one that happened
// to be asked second.
$local->put("shared-name", "the core's own");
$process->forget("shared-name");
var $core = ($local->get("shared-name") ?? "") as string;
echo "the local tier still has: ", $core, "\n";
echo "forgotten on the process tier: ", $process->get("shared-name") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";
--EXPECT--
forgotten on the local tier: null
the process tier still has: the process's own
the local tier still has: the core's own
forgotten on the process tier: null
